\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Even subalgebras of the two forms}
\date{2026-11-12}

\p{Let #{V=ku\oplus W} be a free splitting, with
#{B_q(u,W)=0} for the bilinear form of \ref{fdip-000P} and with
#{q(u)=c} a unit of #{k}. Write #{q^\theta} for the quadratic
form that agrees with #{q} on #{W} and sends #{u} to #{-c}.
The map #{w\mapsto wu} induces a unital homomorphism
##{\operatorname{Cl}(W,c\,q|_W)
 \to\operatorname{Cl}^+(V,q^\theta)}
and a unital homomorphism
##{\operatorname{Cl}(W,-c\,q|_W)
 \to\operatorname{Cl}^+(V,q).}
When #{W} has an orthogonal basis, both maps are
isomorphisms. In particular, on the rank #{4} quotient of
signature #{(3,1)} in \ref{fdip-00Q1}, with #{c=1} and with
an orthogonal basis of that quotient,
##{\operatorname{Cl}^+(V,q^\theta)\cong\operatorname{Cl}(3,0),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{Cl}^+(V,q)\cong\operatorname{Cl}(0,3).}}

\proof{
\p{Use the square relation of \ref{fdip-000P}. Orthogonality
gives #{uw=-wu}. Hence
##{(wu)^2=wuwu=w(-wu)u=-q(w)q(u)=-c\,q(w)}
for the form #{q}. The universal property supplies a unital
homomorphism
#{\operatorname{Cl}(W,-c\,q|_W)\to\operatorname{Cl}(V,q)}
sending #{w} to #{wu}. The image lies in the even subalgebra.
Since #{c} is a unit,
##{ww'=-c^{-1}(wu)(w'u),}
so every product of two vectors in #{W} is in the image, and
so is every product #{uw'=-w'u}. Products of even length are
products of these degree #{2} elements, so the map is
surjective onto #{\operatorname{Cl}^+(V,q)}.}

\p{The same calculation for #{q^\theta}, whose value on #{u}
is #{-c}, replaces #{-c} by #{c} and supplies the first
homomorphism. Take an orthogonal basis of #{W}. Squares and
anticommutation reduce every word to a unique ordered
monomial, so those monomials are a basis of each Clifford
algebra. Ordered monomials in the elements #{e_iu} match the
even monomials of #{V}, up to the unit #{c}. The two bases
have equal rank, and a surjective map of free modules of
equal finite rank is an isomorphism.}
}
